---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { gtBuild, fmt, href, repoUrl } from "../lib/data";

const gt = gtBuild();
const c = gt?.counts ?? {};
const rows: [string, number | undefined][] = [
  ["Participants", c.participants], ["Study days", c.participant_days],
  ["Photos scored (before or package front)", c.images], ["  before photos", c.images_by_type?.before],
  ["  package front photos", c.images_by_type?.front], ["Photos with an after photo or ingredient label", c.companions_present],
  ["ASA24 lines with a photo filename, before missing-file exclusion", c.linked_rows], ["ASA24 lines without a photo filename (excluded)", c.unlinked_rows],
  ["Dishes (lines sharing a FoodNum within a photo)", c.dishes], ["Distinct food codes", c.unique_food_codes],
];
---
<Page title="Data" description="SNAPMe source data, reference counts, exclusions and result files.">
  <h1>Data</h1>
  <p class="lede">SNAPMe (Surveying Nutrient Assessment with Photographs of Meals) links phone photos to
    ASA24 food records entered by the same participants. The study collected diet records over up to three days
    per person. The benchmark uses the public December 2022 release, with the exclusions described below.</p>

  <h2 id="source">Source</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Dataset: Chin, Larke, Bouzid, Nguyen, Vainberg, Smilowitz and Lemay. SNAPMe DB v1.0 (December 9, 2022). Ag Data
      Commons. <a href="https://doi.org/10.15482/USDA.ADC/1528346">doi:10.15482/USDA.ADC/1528346</a>. CC BY-SA 4.0.</li>
    <li>Paper: Larke JA, Chin EL, Bouzid YY, Nguyen T, Vainberg Y, Lee DH, Pirsiavash H, Smilowitz JT, Lemay DG.
      Surveying Nutrient Assessment with Photographs of Meals (SNAPMe): A Benchmark Dataset of Food Photos for Dietary
      Assessment. <i>Nutrients</i> 2023;15(23):4972. <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/nu15234972">doi:10.3390/nu15234972</a>.</li>
    <li>The paper's analysis code and outputs: <a href="https://github.com/JulesLarke-USDA/SNAPMe">github.com/JulesLarke-USDA/SNAPMe</a>{" "}
      (CC BY-SA 4.0). This benchmark uses a fixed revision of the authors' per-photo word sets and model outputs for the 2023 comparison.</li>
    <li>Food codes: USDA Food and Nutrient Database for Dietary Studies 2017-2018, used by ASA24-2020.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="counts">Counts</h2>
  <p class="caption" id="table-1-caption"><b>Table 1.</b> Reference counts from the release. The linkage count includes the line for a missing photo; photo, dish and distinct-code counts exclude it.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap narrow">
    <table aria-labelledby="table-1-caption">
      <tbody>{rows.map(([k, v]) => <tr><td style={k.startsWith("  ") ? "padding-left:1.6rem" : ""}>{k.trim()}</td><td class="n">{fmt.n(v)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>One photo named in the linkage file is absent from the release. That photo and its linked ASA24 line are
    excluded from scoring. Lines without photo filenames are retained in a separate table and are also excluded
    from photo scores. Counts and exclusions are recorded in <a href={href("data/gt_build.json")}>gt_build.json</a>;
    the category rules are described in <a href={href("methods/#reference")}>Methods, section 2</a>.</p>
  <p class="note">Checksums identify the source files used for the reference build. Release checksum (MD5) {gt?.source?.md5}; linkage file SHA-256 {gt?.source?.linkfile_sha256?.slice(0, 16)}…
    Reference built {gt?.built_utc}.</p>

  <h2 id="files">Files</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={href("data/leaderboard.csv")}>leaderboard.csv</a>: one row per model and method (the <code>pipeline</code> column).</li>
    <li><a href={href("data/leaderboard.json")}>leaderboard.json</a>: the same results with 95% intervals and values by photo type and food-code count.</li>
    <li><a href={href("data/baselines_2023.json")}>baselines_2023.json</a>: Inverse Cooking and Im2Recipe, rescored.</li>
    <li><a href={href("data/nutrition5k.json")}>nutrition5k.json</a>: Nutrition5k scores, one row per model.</li>
    <li>A results release packages per-photo measures, raw replies and reference tables. Instructions for building
      these files from the public inputs are on the <a href={href("reproduce/")}>Reproduce page</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="other">Other datasets</h2>
  <p>For SNAPMe, the benchmark compares food lists estimated by models from participants' phone photos of daily
    meals with the ASA24 lines those participants entered. The additional Nutrition5k evaluation compares food
    lists estimated by models from overhead cafeteria photos with the dishes' weighed ingredients. A camera on
    a fixed rig looked down from directly above each plate. These comparisons
    answer different questions; their references and matching rules differ, so their scores are not directly
    comparable. Other datasets with weighed
    foods or three-dimensional scans are listed in the <a href={repoUrl("docs/ground_truth_datasets.md")}>dataset
    comparison</a>. Their records and scores are kept separate from SNAPMe.</p>
  <h3 id="nutrition5k">Nutrition5k source</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Paper: Quin Thames, Arjun Karpur, Wade Norris, Fangting Xia, Liviu Panait, Tobias Weyand and Jack Sim.
      Nutrition5k: Towards Automatic Nutritional Understanding of Generic Food.{" "}
      <i>2021 IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)</i>, 2021.{" "}
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR46437.2021.00879">doi:10.1109/CVPR46437.2021.00879</a>.
      Preprint: <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.03375">arXiv:2103.03375</a>.</li>
    <li>Dataset released by Google Research. The{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/google-research-datasets/Nutrition5k">Nutrition5k repository</a> is archived and
      read-only. Data files are downloaded from the dataset's Google Cloud Storage bucket,{" "}
      <code>nutrition5k_dataset</code>.</li>
    <li>License: <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
      International (CC BY 4.0)</a>.</li>
    <li>This benchmark scores the overhead color photos of the 507 dishes in the authors' published overhead
      test list, <code>dish_ids/splits/depth_test_ids.txt</code>. The reference keeps ingredient names,
      ingredient grams and total dish mass. Calorie and nutrient fields are not kept.</li>
    <li>No Nutrition5k photos are shown or redistributed on this site. The published Nutrition5k file contains
      scores.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="licenses">Licenses</h2>
  <p>Code: CC0 1.0. Results, derived reference tables and the reduced photo copies used in Explore: CC BY-SA 4.0, as
    derived from SNAPMe. Model weights remain subject to the developer's license, recorded in each model file.</p>
  <p>Nutrition5k results are derived from Nutrition5k (Thames et al. 2021), used under{" "}
    <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>, and are released under CC BY 4.0 with
    that credit.</p>
</Page>
